<Base
  title="Projects — Christopher Uzochukwu"
  description="FleetPilot, Commerce Operations Console, and an open pull request to pressly/goose."
>
  <h1>Projects</h1>
  <p>Two public repositories, and one open-source change.</p>

  <h2>FleetPilot</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/chrisuzor/fleetpilot">Repository</a>
    ·
    <a href="https://github.com/chrisuzor/fleetpilot/actions/workflows/test.yml">CI</a>
    ·
    <a href="https://github.com/chrisuzor/fleetpilot/blob/main/docs/architecture.md">Architecture</a>
    ·
    <a href="/case-studies/fleetpilot/">Case study</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/chrisuzor/fleetpilot/actions/workflows/test.yml">
      <img
        class="badge"
        alt="FleetPilot test workflow status"
        src="https://github.com/chrisuzor/fleetpilot/actions/workflows/test.yml/badge.svg"
        width="128"
        height="20"
      />
    </a>
  </p>
  <h3>Problem</h3>
  <p>
    Desired state and the provider can disagree, and two workers can try to create the same name.
  </p>
  <h3>Architecture</h3>
  <p>
    PostgreSQL stores the requested fleet, jobs, and an audit log. A reconciler enqueues the
    difference. A worker applies it to a fake in-process provider. The case study has the diagram.
  </p>
  <h3>Engineering decision</h3>
  <p>
    The instance lock is <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code>. A held row does not block the second
    applier and does not spend a retry. Provider failures use a short budget, then the job is dead.
  </p>
  <h3>Failure mode</h3>
  <p>
    A provider error is retried at 100 ms, 200 ms, 400 ms, and 800 ms. The fifth failure marks the
    job dead. A rolling update pauses on that dead job, or on an unhealthy check, and does not move
    the next name.
  </p>
  <h3>Testing</h3>
  <p><code>go test -race ./...</code> on every push. The tests do not start PostgreSQL.</p>
  <h3>Stack</h3>
  <p>Go, PostgreSQL, queues, OpenTelemetry, and Kubernetes manifests for a local layout.</p>
  <h3>Limitations</h3>
  <p>
    The provider does not boot a machine or call a cloud API. The role header is not a credential.
    The image is not pushed, and the OpenTofu module is validated rather than applied.
  </p>

  <h2>Commerce Operations Console</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/chrisuzor/commerce-ops-console">Repository</a>
    ·
    <a href="https://github.com/chrisuzor/commerce-ops-console/actions/workflows/ci.yml">CI</a>
    ·
    <a href="https://github.com/chrisuzor/commerce-ops-console/blob/main/ARCHITECTURE.md">Architecture</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/chrisuzor/commerce-ops-console/actions/workflows/ci.yml">
      <img
        class="badge"
        alt="Commerce Operations Console CI status"
        src="https://github.com/chrisuzor/commerce-ops-console/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg"
        width="100"
        height="20"
      />
    </a>
  </p>
  <h3>Problem</h3>
  <p>
    Failed commerce orders need a record of what payment, fulfillment, and notification systems
    already did, including deliveries that arrive twice or out of order.
  </p>
  <h3>Architecture</h3>
  <p>
    Events are an append-only history. A signed webhook or a GraphQL mutation accepts them. Order
    state is rebuilt from those events. Operators can retry fulfillment or acknowledge an issue.
    PostgreSQL is one mode. A seeded in-memory store is the other.
  </p>
  <h3>Engineering decision</h3>
  <p>
    Idempotency keys collapse duplicate deliveries. The incident summary is built only from stored
    events.
  </p>
  <h3>Failure mode</h3>
  <p>
    A paid order that was never fulfilled, or a customer who was never notified. A repeated
    delivery must not apply twice.
  </p>
  <h3>Testing</h3>
  <p>
    <code>make test</code> runs the Go and React tests. <code>make lint</code> runs
    <code>go vet</code> and ESLint. <code>make build</code> builds the frontend and the Go binary.
    CI runs those checks.
  </p>
  <h3>Stack</h3>
  <p>Go, GraphQL, PostgreSQL, React, and TypeScript.</p>
  <h3>Limitations</h3>
  <p>
    It is a portfolio sample, not a hosted order processor, and it is not the permission-aware
    investigation workflow in the incident case study. The repository says authentication,
    authorization, retention, migration tooling, and external fulfillment adapters are not in this
    sample.
  </p>

  <h2>Goose: UTF-8 BOM before a migration directive</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/pressly/goose/pull/1157">Pull request 1157</a>
    ·
    <a href="https://github.com/pressly/goose">pressly/goose</a>
  </p>
  <h3>Problem</h3>
  <p>
    A SQL migration saved with a UTF-8 byte order mark fails to parse. The first line looks like
    <code>-- +goose Up</code>, but <code>strings.TrimSpace</code> does not remove U+FEFF, so the
    opening annotation never matches.
  </p>
  <h3>Architecture</h3>
  <p>
    The change is in the SQL parser. It strips one leading byte order mark from the first scanned
    line, before the annotation check.
  </p>
  <h3>Engineering decision</h3>
  <p>
    Strip one leading UTF-8 BOM and leave UTF-16 byte order marks unchanged.
  </p>
  <h3>Failure mode</h3>
  <p>
    The parser reported <code>unexpected state 0</code> on the line
    <code>-- +goose Up</code> when a UTF-8 BOM was present.
  </p>
  <h3>Testing</h3>
  <p>
    <code>TestUTF8ByteOrderMark</code> reproduces that case. The pull request says the test failed
    with <code>unexpected state 0</code> before the change and passes after it. The command in the
    commit is <code>go test ./internal/sqlparser/ -count=1</code>.
  </p>
  <h3>Stack</h3>
  <p>Go.</p>
  <h3>Limitations</h3>
  <p>
    The pull request is open. It does not show a completed status check. UTF-16 behaviour is
    intentionally preserved. This is not a merged release.
  </p>
</Base>
